# The one door.
#
# ADR-0005 §1: Caddy is the only address anybody types. `/` is the scanner,
# `/docs` the portal, `/cabinet` the cabinet, and `/v0/*` and `/x402/*`
# are both the gateway — the merchant's API and the storefront an agent buys at,
# less three calls only the cabinet makes from inside the stack — so a
# merchant's engineer sees one origin and never reasons about ports. The
# same file describes the server later, which is why nothing below is written
# twice: every address the routes depend on is one environment variable with a
# local default, and the deployment overrides the variable rather than editing
# a copy of this file.
#
#   AGENTIFY_SITE_ADDRESS      what Caddy listens on              :8080
#   AGENTIFY_FRONT_PAGE        required front-page route          no default
#   AGENTIFY_ASSET_ROOT        the shared browser assets          /srv/assets
#   AGENTIFY_PORTAL_ROOT       the portal's built output          /srv/docs
#   AGENTIFY_CABINET_UPSTREAM  host:port of the cabinet process   cabinet:3001
#   AGENTIFY_GATEWAY_UPSTREAM  host:port of the gateway process   gateway:3000
#   AGENTIFY_SCANNER_UPSTREAM  host:port of the scanner process   scanner:3000
#   AGENTIFY_SURFACE_MODE      what the static pages say           sandbox
#
# The defaults are the container's view of the world, because ADR-0005 §7 wants
# the whole chain to come up from `docker compose up`, and it does: the compose
# file in this repository brings up all of it, and the services these defaults
# name are the service names on its network. Running this file from a checkout
# outside Docker still works, with the roots pointed at the repository:
#
#   AGENTIFY_FRONT_PAGE=scanner_front_page \
#   AGENTIFY_ASSET_ROOT=packages/visual/public \
#   AGENTIFY_PORTAL_ROOT=apps/docs/.vitepress/dist \
#   AGENTIFY_CABINET_UPSTREAM=127.0.0.1:3001 \
#   AGENTIFY_GATEWAY_UPSTREAM=127.0.0.1:3000 \
#   AGENTIFY_SCANNER_UPSTREAM=127.0.0.1:3000 \
#     caddy run --config deploy/Caddyfile
#
# The listen address defaults to a high port rather than :80 so that the same
# line works unprivileged on a laptop and inside a container. A deployment sets
# AGENTIFY_SITE_ADDRESS to its hostname, and Caddy's automatic HTTPS turns
# itself on because the address stops being a bare port — which is the reason
# this file does not disable it.

# A direct test deployment receives TLS through an SNI pass-through ingress;
# port 80 stops at that ingress. TLS-ALPN therefore reaches this Caddy while
# HTTP-01 does not. The production inner Caddy listens on a bare private port,
# so this issuer block is inert there and the public edge owns certificates.
{
	# A failed upstream also logs a request through the runtime logger. Access
	# log filtering alone cannot protect a one-time link's query or cookies.
	log default {
		format filter {
			request>uri delete
			request>headers delete
			resp_headers delete
			wrap json
		}
	}
	# Only the challenge that can arrive here. Overridable, because a deployment
	# behind a different edge may reach port 80 after all.
	acme_ca {$AGENTIFY_ACME_CA:https://acme-v02.api.letsencrypt.org/directory}
	cert_issuer acme {
		disable_http_challenge
	}
	# A second Caddy may terminate TLS before this one. Trust only that exact
	# peer for X-Forwarded-Proto and X-Forwarded-For; broad private ranges would
	# let another Docker service impersonate a browser's address.
	servers {
		trusted_proxies static {$AGENTIFY_TRUSTED_EDGE_CIDR:127.0.0.1/32}
		trusted_proxies_strict
	}
}

# Every path the site block below leaves unclaimed is the scanner's, `/admin`
# included. The operator's dashboard has no door of its own here: it opens for
# an account that carries the operator flag and answers everybody else with the
# site's 404 page, and the scanner decides which (ADR-0026 §6).
(scanner_front_page) {
	handle {
		reverse_proxy {$AGENTIFY_SCANNER_UPSTREAM:scanner:3000} {
			# Scanner consumers historically disagreed about whether the first or
			# last forwarded address was the browser. Give them one address. The
			# global trusted-proxy boundary above decides it; incoming values from
			# any other peer are not evidence.
			header_up X-Forwarded-For {client_ip}
		}
	}
}

# The mark and the faces the cabinet's HTML asks for. The scanner owns the
# front page and bundles its own copies, so these are Caddy's to serve from the
# visual package. Naming the two marks and the font subtree is deliberate: a
# broad /assets or /styles route would silently reserve scanner namespaces that
# the cabinet does not use.
(shared_assets) {
	@shared_assets path /assets/agentify-mark.svg /assets/agentify-mark-heavy.svg /styles/fonts.css /styles/fonts/*.woff2
	handle @shared_assets {
		root * {$AGENTIFY_ASSET_ROOT:/srv/assets}
		file_server
	}
}

{$AGENTIFY_SITE_ADDRESS::8080} {
	encode zstd gzip

	# The web surface decision puts four things on one origin, and same-origin is
	# then the whole of the boundary between them: the scanner and the reports it
	# writes, a static portal build, a cabinet that holds a merchant's session,
	# and the money path at /v0. These three headers are the part of the
	# hardening that costs nothing.
	#
	# It said "and cannot break a page", and one of them broke every form in the
	# cabinet. `Referrer-Policy: no-referrer` does more than hide the referrer:
	# by the fetch specification, a request whose method is not GET or HEAD and
	# whose referrer policy is `no-referrer` carries `Origin: null` instead of
	# the page's own origin. So every form post from the cabinet arrived claiming
	# to come from nowhere, the check that a form came from this host refused it,
	# and a merchant on the live site could not sign in at all. Nothing about it
	# looked like a header problem: the same request from a command line, which
	# implements no referrer policy, was let through.
	#
	# `same-origin` gives up nothing we wanted. A referrer still never reaches
	# another site, which is the whole reason the line is here, and our own
	# origin survives on our own requests. `no-referrer` must not come back.
	#
	# What is deliberately not here: HSTS, because on a plain-HTTP local origin
	# it would pin localhost in every developer's browser for its max-age; and a
	# Content-Security-Policy, because the portal's build and the cabinet's
	# inline scripts have not been surveyed and a policy written blind either
	# breaks pages or is loose enough to be decoration. Both belong with the
	# first deployment to a real host, and neither should be guessed here.
	header {
		X-Content-Type-Options nosniff
		Referrer-Policy same-origin
		X-Frame-Options DENY
	}

	# The mail-link form keeps its token out of Referer, including requests to
	# this origin. Unlike no-referrer, strict-origin preserves the Origin of a
	# native form POST, so the cabinet can keep rejecting cross-origin submits.
	@cabinet_link path /cabinet/sign-in/open

	import shared_assets

	# The gateway, at both of the prefixes the contract's own route table is
	# written under (packages/contracts/src/api.ts): /v0 is the merchant's API
	# and /x402 is the storefront — the catalog an agent browses, the address it
	# buys at, and the door it comes back to for its own order. Both are the
	# gateway's, not mount points invented here: the path is passed through
	# untouched and the process behind it sees exactly the URL the agent asked
	# for. A matcher that carried only /v0 would leave every storefront address
	# falling through to the scanner below, which answers its own 404 — so a
	# product listed in a discovery catalog would be unbuyable from outside
	# while every test in this repository stayed green.
	#
	# The gateway must be started with PUBLIC_BASE_URL set to this origin. It
	# puts that address into the payment challenge it sends an agent, and an
	# agent that is told to come back to the process's own port would be sent
	# at a door that is closed from outside.
	#
	# /healthz is in the same matcher, and it is deliberately not under /v0.
	# Whether this stack is up has to be answerable from outside the door, and
	# from outside there was nothing to ask: the path fell through to the front
	# page, which answers a 404 to a name it does not have, and /v0/healthz
	# arrived at the gateway as a route the contract does not carry and was
	# refused as one. Three of the four things on this origin are processes and
	# the fourth is a directory this server reads itself, so the health of the
	# door is the gateway's — it is what the money path runs through, and each
	# of the others answers for itself or is answered for by Caddy being up at
	# all. The path stays outside /v0 because that table is the contract a
	# merchant's engineer integrates against, and an operational probe is not
	# part of it.
	#
	# Who can ask is worth being plain about, because on a deployed origin this
	# path is public: there is no door in front of it, so anybody who knows the
	# hostname can ask whether the gateway is up, and a monitor that has to be
	# given a credential first is a monitor nobody sets up. What that gives away
	# is the answer itself and nothing else — the gateway replies `{"ok":true}`
	# and carries no version, no hostname, no counts and no error text — so what
	# leaks is that this address exists, which the rest of the origin already
	# says.
	#
	# What comes back is the gateway's own answer and nothing more. No verdict
	# here stands for the cabinet, and none stands for Postgres — a gateway
	# answering this route has not been asked whether its database is reachable,
	# so `{"ok":true}` here is compatible with a stack that cannot serve a single
	# order. A document that averaged them would be read as one and would be
	# wrong the first time one of them went down alone; the services that know
	# their own state report it to compose, which is where a per-service verdict
	# belongs.
	#
	# The cabinet is the second process and needs no line of its own. It answers
	# the same probe under its own prefix, and the block below passes /cabinet/*
	# through untouched, so /cabinet/healthz already arrives.
	#
	# Three of the gateway's calls are not on this door at all: registering a
	# merchant, and making and forgetting the key a cabinet calls with. Only the
	# cabinet makes them, and it makes them over the stack's own network, at the
	# gateway's service name, never through here. A merchant comes into being
	# one way, when a signed-in person presses the cabinet's one control
	# (ADR-0014), and the registration call behind that press asks for nothing
	# but the invitation, which lives in configuration files; reachable from
	# outside, it would make a merchant and its cabinet's key for whoever holds
	# a copy, with no address and no person. So these paths are left out of the
	# gateway's matcher and fall through to the front page, which answers them
	# as it answers any path this site does not have, with its missing page.
	# The matcher is the same one the gateway's line uses, so a path spelled in
	# another case, encoded, or with a dot segment or a doubled slash is left
	# out exactly when the gateway's line would have taken it.
	@gateway {
		path /v0 /v0/* /x402 /x402/* /healthz
		not path /v0/merchants /v0/merchants/* /v0/keys/cabinet /v0/keys/cabinet/*
	}
	handle @gateway {
		reverse_proxy {$AGENTIFY_GATEWAY_UPSTREAM:gateway:3000}
	}

	# The cabinet, its own process rather than part of the gateway (ADR-0005
	# §2). The prefix is passed through rather than stripped, deliberately: the
	# cabinet then reads its own mount point off the request it is answering
	# and builds its links from the same string, instead of being told the
	# prefix out of band and having to prepend it to every href. So the cabinet
	# routes on /cabinet, /cabinet/orders, /cabinet/receipts as written.
	#
	# The prefix is spelled as two exact paths rather than one /cabinet* glob so
	# that it claims the cabinet and its pages and nothing else: a scanner page
	# at /cabinets-we-have-known would otherwise disappear into the proxy.
	@cabinet path /cabinet /cabinet/*
	handle @cabinet {
		header @cabinet_link Referrer-Policy strict-origin
		reverse_proxy {$AGENTIFY_CABINET_UPSTREAM:cabinet:3001}
	}

	# The portal. Here the prefix IS stripped, because the other side is a
	# directory of files and not a process: /docs/money has to become money.html
	# on disk.
	#
	# That cuts both ways. The built HTML asks for its stylesheets by absolute
	# path and links its own pages the same way, so the portal has to be built
	# knowing where it lives:
	#
	#   pnpm --dir apps/docs exec vitepress build --base /docs/
	#
	# Built without that flag, every page comes out asking for /assets/... at
	# the root of the origin, where the scanner answers and has nothing by that
	# name, and every link in the sidebar points outside /docs. The flag is a
	# build argument rather than an edit to the portal's config on purpose: the
	# mount point is this file's business, and the portal should not have to
	# know it in a file of its own.
	# Temporary rather than permanent on purpose: a 301 is cached by the browser
	# past the life of the mount point, and the first person to move /docs would
	# spend an afternoon on it.
	redir /docs /docs/
	handle_path /docs/* {
		root * {$AGENTIFY_PORTAL_ROOT:/srv/docs}

		# cleanUrls is on in the portal's config, so /docs/money is a request
		# for a page whose file carries the extension.
		try_files {path} {path}.html {path}/index.html
		# Which stack this is, and the only place in this system where the mode
		# is a variable rather than a derivation: Caddy cannot read a CAIP-2
		# identifier and cannot hold the list of chains. Two fields can
		# disagree, so the release checks this one against the channel before
		# anything starts and against the rendered pages after they are up.
		#
		# The delimiters are comment-shaped so that these files are readable
		# and openable without this server in front of them, and `mime` keeps
		# the templating off everything that is not a page — a stylesheet or a
		# built script containing two braces is not a template.
		templates {
			mime text/html
			between <!--{{ }}-->
		}
		file_server
	}

	# The portal is a directory of files, so a page that is not there would be
	# Caddy's bare, empty 404. VitePress builds its own 404 page under the same
	# base, and this serves it with the status kept. The scanner and the
	# cabinet answer their own missing pages, so nothing here touches theirs.
	#
	# The 404 page is a portal page like any other, so it is templated the same
	# way. Served without it, the page carries every mode's notice at once, and
	# a live deployment tells whoever mistyped an address that its payments are
	# pretend.
	handle_errors {
		@portal_missing `{err.status_code} == 404 && {http.request.orig_uri.path}.startsWith('/docs/')`
		handle @portal_missing {
			root * {$AGENTIFY_PORTAL_ROOT:/srv/docs}
			rewrite * /404.html
			templates {
				mime text/html
				between <!--{{ }}-->
			}
			file_server {
				status {err.status_code}
			}
		}
	}

	# One route table, three stacks. The selector has no default, so a stack
	# that names no front page is refused at load rather than answering a bare
	# 404 at its own address.
	import {$AGENTIFY_FRONT_PAGE}
}
